{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "gpt-6-sol",
  "run": 6,
  "simulation_id": "sim-05",
  "member_id": "pearce-mark",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B035",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B034",
    "B009"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. B035 remains the most clearly bounded prize-sized experimental advance: magic-wavelength confinement created a new regime in which many atoms can be held for precision optical spectroscopy without the central motional limitation, and sustained independent performance demonstrates that this is a durable physical measurement capability rather than a single laboratory result. Katori's originating concept and Ye's decisive experimental development remain a compact, intelligible division of credit. The citation should recognize the established invention and realization of the platform itself, not depend on a future redefinition of the second or on speculative applications.",
    "case_against": "The larger optical-clock community made important contributions to implementation and systematic control, so the historical basis for limiting credit to Katori and Ye must remain exact. Moreover, the most conspicuous fundamental discoveries enabled by lattice clocks are still developing. These reservations argue for a narrow citation and careful credit review, but do not outweigh the demonstrated maturity, reproducibility, and conceptual clarity of the core achievement.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig argues that B009 merits priority because KPZ supplies an experimentally verified universal law, whereas optical lattice clocks are principally an enabling platform whose eventual discoveries remain partly prospective.",
        "response": "I accept the force of the distinction and regard KPZ as an excellent alternative. But an experimental invention need not await a downstream discovery when it has already opened a qualitatively new, independently reproducible measurement regime. For B035, the central advance is not simply better timekeeping: it is the demonstrated ability to control optical atomic spectroscopy at a level that makes time, gravity, and fundamental tests quantitatively accessible. Its attribution is presently more compact than the required division between KPZ formulation and later experimental verification."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson maintains that B024 is a mature, transformative instrumental invention, while emphasizing that priority among correction concepts, practical implementations, and commercial engineering must be resolved.",
        "response": "I agree that aberration-corrected electron optics has transformed what can be observed and that its instrumental character is no objection in principle. The remaining allocation issue is decisive in comparing it with B035: B024 requires us to separate several correction concepts and implementation paths before we can be confident that Rose, Urban, and Haider exhaust the prize-worthy credit. By contrast, the proposed Katori-Ye pairing maps more directly onto the central lattice-clock concept and its foundational realization, subject to the same necessary archival review."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson contends that inflation and the quantum origin of primordial structure have broad observational support despite the absence of an identified microscopic inflationary mechanism.",
        "response": "The observational success of the inflationary framework is profound, and B027 remains an intellectually compelling alternative. I remain reluctant to formulate a prize citation that could be read as confirming a particular physical mechanism when the evidence does not select one. B035 permits a narrower statement of an experimentally established achievement with direct reproducibility and a more delimited two-person attribution."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether historical review confirms that Katori and Ye alone encompass the decisive conceptual and foundational experimental contributions to optical lattice clocks.",
      "Whether a final citation can remain strictly confined to the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks while fairly distinguishing broader optical-clock development and systematic-control work."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
